# Home Assistant interface coverage

Use this page to find the task pattern, component, style and example for any Home Assistant interface entry. Every entry in the registries below has all four. This is **design coverage of a defined source baseline**. It does not show that every Home Assistant integration has been redesigned, that every native control has passed browser testing, or that an example installs a native adapter.

<% inventory = JSON.parse(File.read(File.join(root, 'data', 'ha-interface-coverage.json'))) %>

<h2 id="baseline">Coherent version baseline</h2>

This inventory was reviewed on 12 November 2026 against Home Assistant Core **<%= inventory['baseline']['core_version'] %>** and frontend **<%= inventory['baseline']['frontend_version'] %>**. Core's frontend manifest pins that exact frontend release. Public documentation is separately fixed to the website commit recorded in the [machine-readable inventory](../reference/ha-interface-coverage.json).

The <%= inventory['totals']['entries'] %> records belong to <%= inventory['totals']['categories'] %> overlapping registries. They are **not <%= inventory['totals']['entries'] %> unique user-visible components**. A public card, its source dispatcher key and its domain control, for example, each have their own record. Internal routes, legacy aliases and reserved entries are labelled, and none is recommended as new public configuration.

<h2 id="scope">What is included</h2>

- Every public dashboard card catalogue entry, all four native view layouts and every pinned card dispatcher key.
- Every registered card feature, every closed feature style variant, Tile placement, badges, header/footer types and picture elements.
- Every entity/special-row dispatcher key, domain-to-row mapping and dedicated More info control file.
- Every public selector, native selector dispatcher key and explicit legacy selector alias.
- All Core entity platforms; additional helper/state domains are separately limited to those referenced by the native row/dialog registries.
- Native device-control primitives and shared user-facing primitive families, with their exact source members.
- Top-level panel families, Settings routes, developer tools and native external settings destinations.

Integration-specific modes, ranges, attributes, media sources and permissions stay dynamic, and a fixed inventory cannot supply their values. A custom card, More info control, panel or integration needs its own reviewed extension record. Source utility functions, CSS-only helpers, animation machinery and instrumentation are not separate public components.

<h2 id="interpretation">How to read a mapping</h2>

Each mapping has four links. The task pattern gives the behaviour and the limits of what is known. The component is the reusable visual and interaction owner. The style gives the visual roles. The example shows an invented household scenario. An example can stand for a whole family of entries, but it does not copy the native component’s source or every capability variant.

A native interface still needs its own acceptance record for a stated version. Check the [Compatibility register and native fallback](compatibility-register.html), [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html) and the [Home Assistant coverage and terminology audit](../community/home-assistant-coverage-audit.html). Keep native translations, user-supplied names, values, event handling and permissions. A theme cannot add missing application behaviour through CSS.

